<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Newcomen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1664">1664</a>–<a href="/years/1729">1729</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Newcomen was an English inventor, ironmonger and Baptist preacher who created the atmospheric engine in 1712. Born in Dartmouth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>, he made and sold tools for the mining industry at a time when flooding in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> mines was a major problem. Combining the ideas of Thomas Savery and Denis Papin, he used steam condensed in a cylinder to draw down a piston that worked a beam engine and pump, and with his partner John Calley built the first successful engine of this type at the Conygree Coalworks, Tipton. He died in 1729 and was buried at Bunhill Fields; by 1733 about 125 of his engines were operating.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Thomas Newcomen was born in Dartmouth into a merchant family and baptised at St Saviour's Church on 28 February 1664. His ironmonger's business specialised in tools for the mining industry. He was a lay preacher and teaching elder in the local Baptist church, and after 1710 he became pastor of a local group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baptists">Baptists</a>. His Baptist connections helped to spread his engine: the engineers Jonathan Hornblower Sr. and his son belonged to the Baptist church at Bromsgrove with which he was linked, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> business contact, Edward Wallin, was a Baptist minister.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Newcomen developed his engine around 1712 to lift water out of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> mine, combining ideas from Thomas Savery and Denis Papin. Savery had devised a fire engine in which steam was admitted to an empty container and condensed, and the vacuum sucked water up from the sump; it could not work beyond a depth of about thirty feet. Newcomen replaced the receiving vessel with a cylinder and piston after Papin's design, so that the vacuum drew down the piston instead. The piston worked a beam engine, a wooden beam rocking on a central fulcrum, with a chain on the far side attached to a pump at the bottom of the mine.</p><p>After 1715 the engine business was run through an unincorporated company, the Proprietors of the Invention for Raising Water by Fire, whose committee included Wallin. Little is known of Newcomen's later life. He died at Wallin's house in 1729 and was buried at Bunhill Fields, though the exact site of his grave is unknown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Newcomen's engines were installed under Savery's patent, which was extended by statute until 1733. By then about 125 had been installed in most of the important mining districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the Continent, draining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mines in the Black Country, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwickshire">Warwickshire</a> and near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle upon Tyne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mines in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>, and lead mines in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flintshire">Flintshire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbyshire">Derbyshire</a>. The engine held its place without material change for about 75 years, and John Smeaton improved its mechanical details in the early 1770s.</p><p>It was far from efficient, since much heat was lost when the steam was condensed in the cylinder, which mattered little at collieries with unsaleable small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> but raised costs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>. After 1775 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-watt">James Watt</a>'s separate condenser replaced it where coal was dear. Even so, of over 2,200 engines built in the 18th century only about 450 were Watt's.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thomas Newcomen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
